---
import { url } from '../../lib/url';

interface Props {
  h1: string;
}

const { h1 } = Astro.props;
---
  <h1>{h1}</h1>

<h2 id="resumen">Summary <i class="fas fa-info-circle"></i> </h2>

<p>
    Academic management platform with three roles —student, teacher and administrator— and an
    integrated conversational assistant that answers questions about the platform and about real
    data in the system according to the role of whoever asks, without being able to create, modify or
    grade anything. Final integrative project for the AI course at UNLa.
</p>

<p>
    <strong>GitHub repository <i class="fab fa-github"></i>: </strong>
    <a href="https://github.com/Franco2900/campus-virtual" target="_blank">Campus Virtual</a>
</p>

<h2 id="tecnologias">Technologies used <i class="bi bi-laptop"></i> </h2>

<div class="row">

    <div class="col-3">
        Backend: <br>
        Python <i class="fab fa-python"></i> with the Flask framework
    </div>

    <div class="col-3">
        Database: <br>
        SQLite <i class="fas fa-database"></i> with SQLAlchemy
    </div>

    <div class="col-3">
        Frontend: <br>
        React <i class="fab fa-react"></i> with Vite
    </div>

    <div class="col-3">
        Chatbot: <br>
        LangChain + local Ollama <i class="fas fa-robot"></i>
    </div>

</div>
<br>

<h2 id="descripcion">Full project description <i class="fas fa-align-left"></i> </h2>

<p>
    The system covers twelve use cases that range from account creation and login to grading
    assignments and consulting grades. Each role only sees its own scope: the administrator manages
    courses, users and enrollments; the teacher creates assignments, grades submissions and consults the
    grade matrix; the student submits assignments and sees their grades. Authorization does not live in
    the client: every endpoint validates the session and the role on the server side.
</p>

<p>
    The backend is organised in layers —<code>routes → services → models</code>— and that separation is
    deliberate: business rules live in <code>services/</code>, never in the blueprints or the models, and
    the blueprints only validate the shape of the request. Persistence is SQLite with SQLAlchemy, and the
    schema is versioned with Flask-Migrate/Alembic instead of being recreated on every start. The session
    is a signed Flask cookie with <code>HttpOnly</code> and <code>SameSite=Lax</code>, and the
    decorators <code>@login_required</code> and <code>@require_roles</code> centralise
    the guards.
</p>

<p>
    The frontend is React with Vite, organised by feature: each use case has its components, its
    API client and its pages in <code>src/features/&lt;feature&gt;/</code>. It has its own design system
    with tokens for light and dark and one colour per role, reusable components and a shell with
    navbar, sidebar and protected routes. Server validations are the source of truth; the client
    only sends and displays errors.
</p>

<p>
    The conversational assistant runs against Ollama locally with LangChain and a small model
    (<code>qwen3:4b</code>). Instead of giving it free access to the database, the agent receives
    read-only tools and each one is scoped by session and by role, so a student cannot consult
    another student's grades. History is persisted and each user can delete their own. For the sources
    the administrator uploads, context is retrieved with RAG over ChromaDB and local embeddings. If
    Ollama is not running, the rest of the application keeps working: only the chat answers with a
    friendly message and that turn is not saved.
</p>

<p>
    The project was built with a spec-driven pipeline: each use case was specified, implemented,
    verified with tests, HTTP smoke tests and visual evidence in light and dark, went through review and
    only then was archived. When the last use case closed, the backend had 321 tests and all twelve were
    implemented end to end.
</p>

